{0 Async and background work}

JavaScript APIs return Promises. In workero you can wait for them in direct
style, without chaining callbacks.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let response = Workero.fetch "https://example.com" in
  let body = Workero.Response.body_text response in
  Workero.Response.text (Printf.sprintf "%d bytes\n" (String.length body))

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

[fetch] waits for response headers. [body_text] waits for the body. Other APIs
that return a {!Workero.Promise.t} need an explicit {!Workero.Promise.await}.

{1:running Run independent work together}

Start independent Promises before you await either. If you await each before
starting the next, their delays add up. If you start both first, their waits
can overlap.

{!Workero.Timer.sleep} returns a Promise that resolves after a delay in
milliseconds:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let a = Timer.sleep 100 and b = Timer.sleep 100 in
  Promise.await a;
  Promise.await b;
  Response.text "done\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Both timers start before the first wait. The waits take about 100 ms together,
not 200 ms. To collect results or handle multiple failures, use
{!Workero.Promise.all} or {!Workero.Promise.all_settled}.

{1 Work after the response}

Use {!Workero.Context.wait_until} for work that does not determine the response,
such as a log write. It starts [f] immediately in its own fiber and registers
that fiber's Promise with [ctx.waitUntil]. Synchronous work in [f] runs before
[wait_until] returns. If [f] suspends, the handler can continue and send the
response while [f] waits. [f] can call {!Workero.Promise.await}.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  Context.wait_until ctx (fun () ->
      let response =
        fetch ~method_:Method.POST ~body:"hit" "https://example.com/log"
      in
      print_endline ("logged: " ^ string_of_int (Response.status response)));
  Response.text "ok\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

The [POST] starts before [wait_until] returns, then [fetch] suspends. The
response does not wait for the [POST] to finish. An exception in [f] rejects
its Promise; the runtime logs it, without changing the response.

For HTTP requests, Cloudflare allows all [waitUntil] tasks for the request to
continue for up to 30 seconds after the response is sent or the client
disconnects. They share this time window; unfinished tasks are canceled. An
active response stream has a different lifetime: while the client is connected
and receiving it, the request remains active. See
{{!page-streams}Streams} and {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/context/#waituntil}Cloudflare's waitUntil documentation}.

{1 Handle errors}

{!Workero.Promise.await} raises {!Workero.Js_error} when a JavaScript Promise
rejects. A rejection from OCaml code, such as an exception in [map], [bind], a
[make] executor or an [of_js] decoder, instead re-raises the original OCaml
exception. {!Workero.Promise.error_of_exn} makes such a rejection.

The payload of {!Workero.Js_error} is a {!Workero.Promise.error}, the original
JavaScript rejection reason. JavaScript allows any value, so the type is opaque.

- {!Workero.Promise.error_to_string} converts it with JavaScript [String], for
  example ["Error: nope"].
- {!Workero.Promise.error_of_string} makes a JavaScript [Error] with a message.
- {!Workero.Promise.error_to_js} and {!Workero.Promise.error_of_js} give and
  take the raw value, so a handler can inspect or re-raise it.

Catch a JavaScript rejection with [match ... with exception Js_error reason -> ...]:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let failed = Promise.reject (Promise.error_of_string "boom") in
  match Promise.await failed with
  | () -> Response.text "unreachable\n"
  | exception Js_error reason ->
      Response.text ~status:`Bad_Gateway
        ("rejected: " ^ Promise.error_to_string reason ^ "\n")

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

The response body is [rejected: Error: boom]. An exception that you do not
catch is an error of the handler (see {{!page-handlers}Handlers}).

An exception that escapes at program start-up fails the Worker load. Fix the
exception and rebuild.

{1 Promise}

{!Workero.Promise.t} is the JavaScript [Promise].

- {!Workero.Promise.make} is [new Promise(executor)]. The executor gets
  [resolve] and [reject]. [reject] takes a {!Workero.Promise.error}.
- {!Workero.Promise.resolve} and {!Workero.Promise.reject} are
  [Promise.resolve] and [Promise.reject].
- {!Workero.Promise.await} gives the resolved value.
- {!Workero.Promise.of_js} and {!Workero.Promise.to_js} convert from and to a
  raw JavaScript Promise. See {{!page-interop}JavaScript interop}.

[Promise.t] is backed by [Js_of_ocaml.Promise]. A promise resolved with another
promise is boxed, so ['a t t] is a promise of a promise, which a raw JavaScript
Promise would flatten.

{1 Combinators}

- {!Workero.Promise.map} and {!Workero.Promise.bind} transform the resolved
  value, like [then].
- {!Workero.Promise.all} resolves with every value in order, or rejects with the
  first rejection.
- {!Workero.Promise.race} settles like the first promise to settle.
- {!Workero.Promise.any} resolves with the first value. When all reject, it
  rejects with an [AggregateError].
- {!Workero.Promise.all_settled} resolves when every promise has settled, with a
  [result] for each. It never rejects.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let numbers = Promise.all [ Promise.resolve 1; Promise.resolve 2 ] in
  let total = Promise.map (List.fold_left ( + ) 0) numbers in
  let settled =
    Promise.all_settled
      [ Promise.resolve "ok"; Promise.reject (Promise.error_of_string "no") ]
  in
  let describe = function
    | Ok value -> value
    | Error reason -> Promise.error_to_string reason
  in
  let results = List.map describe (Promise.await settled) in
  Response.text
    (Printf.sprintf "total=%d results=%s\n" (Promise.await total)
       (String.concat "," results))

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 Where you can await}

Await inside a handler registered with {!Workero.serve},
{!Workero.scheduled}, {!Workero.queue}, {!Workero.email}, {!Workero.tail} or
{!Workero.tail_stream} (including the function that [tail_stream] returns), in
a {!Workero.Context.wait_until} function, or in the [~init], [~fetch], [~alarm],
[~rpc], [~websocket_message], [~websocket_close] and [~websocket_error]
handlers of {!Workero.Durable_object.make}. The [~fetch], [~rpc],
[~scheduled], [~queue] and [~tail] handlers of {!Workero.Entrypoint.make} can
await. So can the function given to
{!Workero.Workflow.register} and the callback of {!Workero.Workflow.Step.do_}.
{!Workero.Durable_object.State.wait_until} and
{!Workero.Durable_object.State.block_concurrency_while} also run functions
that can await. Functions called from these fibers can await too. The function
passed to {!Workero.Durable_object.register} to create an object cannot await;
use [~init] for asynchronous initialization. The same holds for the function
passed to {!Workero.Entrypoint.register}. The function passed to
{!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.transaction_sync} cannot await either: it
raises [Invalid_argument], and the transaction rolls back. Use
{!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.val-transaction} to await in a transaction.
Waiting elsewhere fails. A function that JavaScript calls, such as a
[setTimeout] callback, cannot await:

- In a {!Workero.Timer.set_timeout} callback, the callback runs outside the
  handler fiber.
- In a {!Workero.Promise.make} executor, suspending across the JavaScript
  executor frame fails.
- At program start-up, there is no handler fiber, so the Worker fails to load.

To use a callback result, resolve a {!Workero.Promise.t} from the callback and
await it in a handler. See {{!page-how_it_works}How it works} for the suspension
mechanism.
